# The dogfood instance's host-side state. Install as ~/fq-dogfood/.env
# (compose reads it beside compose.yml; it is variable substitution for
# the file, NOT the processes' environment — that is .secrets/env).
#
# What lives here, and only this: the tag the deploy owns, the five facts
# bootstrap.sh reads off the host, where the images come from, and the
# ops scripts' own knobs. NOT the stack's configuration — the daemon's
# limits, a service's settings, the grace period are values in
# compose.yml, in git, delivered by the deploy. A limit does not get
# edited here; it gets changed in compose.yml and merged.

# The build to run: the twelve-hex commit the images are tagged with —
# `fq --version` inside the image reports the same one. Never
# `main-latest` here: that tag moves under a running instance. deploy.sh
# rewrites this on every deploy and rollback; nothing else does.
FQ_TAG=

# Where the images come from (ADR-0035 slice 2). A mirror or a fork's
# registry goes here; the names after the slash are fixed.
# FQ_IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/bricef

# --- notify.sh: where the unattended scripts reach you --------------------
# A shell command, run with the subject as $1 and the body on stdin, for
# anything that needs a human: a deploy, a rollback, a failed deploy or
# backup, a hygiene warning, a deploy deferred for too long. Every message
# is also appended to logs/notify.log. Test it: ./notify.sh --test
#   ntfy:   FQ_NOTIFY_HOOK=curl -fsS -H "Title: $1" -d @- https://ntfy.sh/<your-topic>
#   Slack:  FQ_NOTIFY_HOOK=jq -Rs --arg s "$1" '{text: ("*" + $s + "*\n" + .)}' | curl -fsS -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d @- https://hooks.slack.com/services/<path>
#   mail:   FQ_NOTIFY_HOOK=mail -s "$1" you@example.com
# FQ_NOTIFY_HOOK=

# deploy.sh --auto reports once when the same build has been deferred
# (an invocation in flight, or a daemon that cannot be asked) for this
# many hours: a stuck invocation or an unpaired container otherwise keeps
# every merge off the host with nothing but a quiet line in the log.
# FQ_DEFER_WARN_HOURS=6

# --- hygiene.sh (every 30 min from the crontab) ------------------------
# Warn (non-zero exit, so cron mails) when the disk docker lives on is
# fuller than this percentage.
# FQ_DISK_WARN_PCT=80
# Above this many gigabytes the daemon's build/ subtree — cargo target,
# sccache, go caches; all regenerable — is emptied, but only while no
# invocation is in flight. The next build after that is cold.
# FQ_BUILD_CACHE_MAX_GB=60
# Warn when the newest backup set is older than this many hours — the
# nightly backup.sh has quietly stopped, or never started.
# FQ_BACKUP_STALE_HOURS=36

# --- backup.sh (nightly from the crontab) ---------------------------------
# Where backup sets go (a directory per set: fq-data.tgz, nats-data.tgz,
# SHA256SUMS, MANIFEST). A mounted remote works; otherwise use the hook.
# FQ_BACKUP_DIR=/home/fq/fq-dogfood/backups
# How many sets to keep on the host.
# FQ_BACKUP_KEEP=7
# A command run with the finished set's directory as its argument — the
# off-host copy (rclone, scp, an object-store CLI). The on-host copy alone
# does not survive the host.
# FQ_BACKUP_HOOK=

# --- the ops service (ADR-0036): this host, as the scheduler sees it ------
# bootstrap.sh fills these five (and appends them to an .env that predates
# them); they describe the host, never the stack, and compose refuses to
# start the `ops` service — or, for the last one, the daemon — without them.
# This directory's absolute path. The ops container mounts it at the same
# path, so `docker compose` in there reads this file and every host path a
# script hands to `docker run -v` resolves.
FQ_DOGFOOD=
# The deploy user's uid and the docker group's gid: the ops container runs
# as them — the posture the host crontab had, no more.
FQ_UID=
FQ_DOCKER_GID=
# This host's short name, for the notifications' signature (a container's
# own hostname is a random id).
FQ_HOST=
# The address other machines reach this host on: compose publishes the
# daemon's edge there as well as on loopback, for an `fq` elsewhere (the
# metrics extractor, a laptop). bootstrap.sh takes the source address of
# the default route; on a host with more than one address, pick — on the
# dogfood guest it is the internal one, the same as DASH_INTERNAL_ADDR.
FQ_EDGE_ADDR=
